  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Reflexive Verbs in the Present",
      "content": "Reflexive verbs describe actions that the subject does to themselves. They are formed with a reflexive pronoun (me, te, se, nos, os, se) + the verb conjugation.\n\nReflexive pronouns:\n- me (myself)\n- te (yourself, informal)\n- se (himself/herself/itself/yourselves formal)\n- nos (ourselves)\n- os (yourselves, Spain informal)\n- se (themselves/yourselves formal)\n\nExample: 'Me despierto a las seis' (I wake up at six).\n\nConjugation of 'despertarse' in the present:\n- yo me despierto\n- tú te despiertas\n- él/ella/usted se despierta\n- nosotros nos despertamos\n- vosotros os despertáis\n- ellos/ellas/ustedes se despiertan",
      "table": {
        "headers": [
          "Pronoun",
          "Despertarse",
          "Levantarse",
          "Ducharse"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "yo",
            "me despierto",
            "me levanto",
            "me ducho"
          ],
          [
            "tú",
            "te despiertas",
            "te levantas",
            "te duchas"
          ],
          [
            "él/ella/usted",
            "se despierta",
            "se levanta",
            "se ducha"
          ],
          [
            "nosotros",
            "nos despertamos",
            "nos levantamos",
            "nos duchamos"
          ],
          [
            "vosotros",
            "os despertáis",
            "os levantáis",
            "os ducháis"
          ],
          [
            "ellos/ellas/ustedes",
            "se despiertan",
            "se levantan",
            "se duchan"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "examples": [
        "Me despierto a las seis de la mañana. (I wake up at six in the morning.)",
        "Ella se ducha después de levantarse. (She showers after getting up.)",
        "Nosotros nos acostamos tarde los fines de semana. (We go to bed late on weekends.)",
        "¿Te afeitas por la mañana o por la noche? (Do you shave in the morning or at night?)"
      ],
      "aid_note": "Romanian reflexive verbs also use pronouns (mă, te, se, ne, vă, se) before the verb: 'mă trezeam' = 'me despertaba'. The structure is the same — just swap the verb conjugation."
    }
  ],
  "pronunciation": [
    {
      "title": "The Rolled R vs Single R",
      "content": "Spanish has a single tap /r/ and a rolled trill /rr/. Understanding the difference is key to being understood.\n\nSingle /r/ (tap — one quick flash of the tongue):\n- Between vowels: caro (expensive), perro (dog)\n- At the start of a word: rojo (red), ramo (bunch)\n- After consonants: azul (blue)\n\nDouble /rr/ or R at the start of a word (trill — multiple rapid flashes):\n- Between vowels: perro (dog), cerveza (beer)\n- At the start: rojo (red), Romeo\n\nTip: The rolled R requires airflow — place your tongue tip behind your upper teeth and blow air. It's like the sound a motor makes.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "perro",
          "source": "dog (rolled R at start)"
        },
        {
          "target": "cerveza",
          "source": "beer (double R = rolled)"
        },
        {
          "target": "cara",
          "source": "face (single tap between vowels)"
        },
        {
          "target": "rojo",
          "source": "red (rolled R at start)"
        }
      ],
      "aid_note": "Romanian also has a single 'r' sound that's similar to the Spanish tap. The rolled trill is an extra skill — practice with words like 'perro' (dog) vs 'pero' (but)."
    }
  ],
